e5d2c236389683982901ad9d.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = -3x + 5$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4ec0d10-9ecf-55db-812c-8c2781886239" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481541 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -3x + 5\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -3x + 5\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-3\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $-3$ that passes through the point $(0,3)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (3) &= -3(x - (0)) \\\\\ny &= -3x + (3) \\\\\ny &= -3x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481526 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481542 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/95/95f066686cfcee7d1bbb9c451bd6ea007c8bb5433bee6eda06f886d241280180.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = 3x + 3$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5797ea3-bc2d-51fe-9adc-fde728d4e47d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481542 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = 3x + 3\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = 3x + 3\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n3\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $3$ that passes through the point $(0,1)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (1) &= 3(x - (0)) \\\\\ny &= 3x + (1) \\\\\ny &= 3x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481544 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481516 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481544 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481544 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e7/e73ff4c357b70c3c740666cab209f33cf08236f9244f044009905a6123cff6b4.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y =-\\frac{3}{2}x$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481544 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc76128b-1dbf-5fe8-8ff5-5650472a624c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481544 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $-\\frac{3}{2}$ that passes through the point $(-2,1)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (1) &= -\\frac{3}{2}(x - (-2)) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{3}{2}x - 3 + (1) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{3}{2}x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481545 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186175910.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481545 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "efc79b25-de1d-5f31-994e-0a7d81b23c65" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481551 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481547 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481551 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481551 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-4,1)$ and is perpendicular to $5x+y=2?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481551 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52c85608-74fb-537e-b49a-ca47a66774d5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481551 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the equation $5 x + y=2$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 x + y=2 \\\\ y &= 2 -5 x \\\\ y &= 2 -5 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $-5,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{5}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-4,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(1) &= \\frac{1}{5}(x-(-4)) \\\\ y-(1) &= \\frac{x}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ y &= \\frac{x}{5} + \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481556 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481548 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481556 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481556 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2, 6)$ and $(-3, 1)$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481556 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80cf21cd-1688-5baa-bf78-1e05cbe212c4" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481556 #edb/ref 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(2,6)$ and $(-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m_1 &= \\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \\\\ &= \\frac{1-(6)}{-3-(2)} \\\\ &= \\frac{-5}{-5} \\\\ &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-1$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(4) &= -1(x-(-3)) \\\\ y-(4) &= -x -3 \\\\ y &= 1 -x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 1 -x \\\\ x + y &= 1 \\\\ 1(x + y) &= 1(1) \\\\ x + y &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481557 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481560 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481560 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(5,6)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7aa6290-5c44-5145-bf1e-dc13974023ae" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481560 #edb/ref 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m_1 &= \\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \\\\ &= \\frac{-1-(5)}{-3-(1)} \\\\ &= \\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(5,6).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(6) &= -\\frac{2}{3}(x-(5)) \\\\ y-(6) &= \\frac{10}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= \\fra