_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 - (-1)}{5 - (2)} \\\\\n&= -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(3,4)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (4) &= -\\frac{1}{3}(x - (3)) \\\\\ny - (4) &= 1 -\\frac{x}{3} \\\\\ny &= 5 -\\frac{x}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487501 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487499 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487501 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487501 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-3, 3)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(2, -1)$ and $(4, 0)$.$y =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487501 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "998bca3d-674e-50cd-9fff-229848ef3e23" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487501 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(2,-1)$ and $(4,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 - (-1)}{4 - (2)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(-3,3)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (3) &= \\frac{1}{2}(x - (-3)) \\\\\ny - (3) &= \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{9}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487508 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487503 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487503 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0, 4)$ and is parallel to the