{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-3 - (-1)}{4 - (2)} \\\\\n&= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n-1\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(1,3)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (3) &= -1(x - (1)) \\\\\ny - (3) &= 1 -x \\\\\ny &= 4 -x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487515 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186175911.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487515 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "d47ff792-418b-5c44-9b43-4ce4ced2163a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490396 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490395 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490396 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490396 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,3)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490396 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6fe3232-91eb-5c5b-90f0-5931f279e031" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490396 #edb/ref 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{1+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (1)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-1.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-1(x-0) \\\\ y-1 & =-x \\\\ y & =1 -x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490397 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186175923.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592